---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Aesthetic Lab | Better Than Awesome Robots" description="Four distinct visual systems for the Better Than Awesome robot project.">
  <header class="site-header">
    <a class="brand" href="/">
      <span class="brand-mark">BTA</span>
      <span>Better Than Awesome</span>
      <span class="brand-sub">Aesthetic Lab</span>
    </a>
    <nav class="nav">
      <a href="/tests/cinematic/">Cinematic</a>\n      <a href="/">Marketplace</a>
      <a href="/tests/procurement/">Procurement</a>
      <a href="/tests/modernist/">Modernist</a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <section class="lab-hero">
    <div class="eyebrow">Aesthetic Lab / 4 live directions</div>
    <h1>Four different ways to sell the future.</h1>
    <p>We are not forcing one design to do every job. The original dark cinematic direction is back, the marketplace remains practical, institutional buyers get a procurement interface, and the severe modernist experiment stays alive as an editorial reference.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="lab-grid">
    <article class="lab-card cinematic">
      <div class="lab-role">Test 00 / Original cinematic direction</div>
      <h2>Dark future tech</h2>
      <p>Deep navy, electric violet, blue and cyan glow, clean white type, restrained futuristic framing, and a premium editorial technology feel.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Original sleek Better Than Awesome direction.</li>
        <li>Robots first, broader future tech beyond robots.</li>
        <li>Minimal glow rather than gamer neon overload.</li>
        <li>Seller handoff appears only when purchase intent begins.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="lab-actions">
        <a class="cine-btn primary" href="/tests/cinematic/">Open Cinematic Test</a>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="lab-card market">
      <div class="lab-role">Test 01 / Main marketplace</div>
      <h2>Better Than Awesome</h2>
      <p>Discovery first. Personality, search, useful categories, product cards, videos and articles, with a deliberate handoff into the seller experience when someone is ready to buy.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Keep the big confident type.</li>
        <li>Keep clean white product merchandising.</li>
        <li>Keep BTA blue and lime as our own identity.</li>
        <li>Borrow seller red only at the buying threshold.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="lab-actions">
        <a class="btn blue" href="/">Open Test 1</a>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="lab-card procurement">
      <div class="lab-role">Test 02 / Procurement</div>
      <h2>Government blue</h2>
      <p>For buyers who want model numbers, task fit, price status, verification dates, RFP language and quote paths instead of editorial enthusiasm.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Keep tables and explicit metadata.</li>
        <li>Keep institutional blue.</li>
        <li>Keep documents and procurement tools visible.</li>
        <li>Keep the independent affiliate disclosure obvious.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="lab-actions">
        <a class="btn" href="/tests/procurement/">Open Test 2</a>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="lab-card modernist">
      <div class="lab-role">Test 03 / Editorial experiment</div>
      <h2>Helvetica documentary energy</h2>
      <p>Radically stripped down. Huge type, negative space, almost no UI decoration. It exists to remind the rest of the system that clarity can be the aesthetic.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Keep the typographic confidence.</li>
        <li>Keep the negative space.</li>
        <li>Keep the dry editorial tone available.</li>
        <li>Do not turn it into a dense store.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="lab-actions">
        <a class="btn" href="/tests/modernist/">Open Test 3</a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="lab-synthesis">
    <div class="eyebrow">What we steal from each</div>
    <h2>Main BTA gets the best parts without becoming a committee.</h2>
    <p>Test 1 remains the public marketplace. From Test 2 we borrow data discipline, verification dates, task fit, quote language and buyer tools. From Test 3 we borrow ruthless typography, whitespace and confidence. We do not flatten the three into one generic design.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
